    {
      "id": 18,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        0,
        -1300
      ],
      "size": [
        1000,
        520
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 2,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## The multilayer perceptron\n\nThe oldest idea in the book and still the default answer when the data is a list of numbers:\n**a stack of Linear layers with something bent between them.**\n\nRead the row from left to right and that is the whole model. 784 pixels in, 256, then 128,\nthen 10 scores — one per digit.\n\nThe bend is the part that matters. Two Linear layers with nothing between them collapse\ninto a single Linear layer, exactly as in example 01, and no amount of depth buys anything.\nThe ReLU is what makes the second layer a *new* function rather than a rearrangement of the\nfirst.\n\nWiden it, deepen it, take the Dropout out — every knob is a widget and every consequence is\none run away."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 19,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1080,
        -1300
      ],
      "size": [
        640,
        520
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 3,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### What flattening costs\n\n`flatten` is on, so each 28x28 image arrives as **784 numbers in a row**. The model has no\nidea that pixel 0 and pixel 1 are next to each other, or that pixel 0 and pixel 28 sits\ndirectly above pixel 0. That structure is simply gone.\n\nIt still gets most of the way there. But compare the summary with example 03, which does\nthe same job on the same 6,000 digits with a convolution:\n\n| | parameters | accuracy |\n|---|---|---|\n| this MLP | 235,146 | 94.1% |\n| the CNN | 105,914 | 98.1% |\n\n**Less than half the weights, and four points better.** Not because convolutions are magic,\nbut because they are told the one thing this model has to discover from scratch: that\nnearby pixels belong together."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 20,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1760,
        -1300
      ],
      "size": [
        900,
        400
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 4,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### Reading the first layer\n\n**View Weights** on `hidden1_1` shows the 256 x 784 matrix the first Linear learned. Each\nrow is one hidden unit's opinion about every pixel — and because the input was a flattened\nimage, a row is really a 28x28 template.\n\nNothing told the model that. It found the structure in the pixels because the structure is\nin the data, which is the more interesting half of what a network does."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
